  "worked_solution": "Let $R$ be the event that the name starts with an \"R,\" and let $E$ be the event that the name starts with an \"E.\" Then the probability that the name will start with an \"R\" or an \"E\" is given by $P(R\\cup E).$\n\nGiven that only one letter can be picked as the first letter, $R$ and $E$ are mutually exclusive events. i.e. $P(R\\cap E)=0.$\n\nThere are $26$ letters in the alphabet, and each letter has an equal chance of being randomly picked ($1$ out of $26$). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =\\frac{1}{26}, \\\\ P(E) & =\\frac{1}{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R\\cup E) & =P(R)+P(E)-P(R\\cap E) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}+\\frac{1}{26}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{26} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
